# A SHELL SCRIPT THAT CROSSES MACHINES MUST KEEP ITS LINE ENDINGS.
#
# This clone runs on Windows with `core.autocrlf=true` and no .gitattributes, so
# every checkout rewrote `scripts/b12-preflight-mac.sh` with CRLF in the working
# tree while the committed blob stayed LF. Measured here: a `git stash` round
# trip turned an LF file into 699 CR bytes. Git Bash tolerates that; bash on
# macOS matches a heredoc terminator by exact string, so `JS\r` never closes a
# `<<'JS'` block and the embedded merge/probe scripts run to end of file.
#
# The Mac script is copied out of a Windows working tree by hand — Google Drive,
# not `git clone` — which is precisely the path that ships the working-tree bytes
# rather than the committed ones. Pinning it here makes the hazard unreachable
# instead of remembered.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reason, one step removed: this one is executed on the Mac by node, which
# does not care about CR — but it is read alongside the shell script and diffed
# against it, and a file that changes shape on checkout is a file whose sha256
# means something different on each machine. B12 pins hashes.
*.mjs text eol=lf

# THE APPEND-ONLY FILE AND THE HASHED EVIDENCE. `MEASUREMENTS.jsonl` guarantees
# that its committed prefix stays byte-identical, and `evidence/*.json` is frozen
# by sha256 — `evidence/2026-08-05-b12-preregistration.json` is pinned at
# 5d42b19a899d4cc4538fcdb4d7573d17cf8ca9d4cb557f148c1746e6181ea55e. A checkout
# that rewrote either with CRLF would break the first invariant on the next
# append and the second on the next verification, on one machine and not the
# other. Both invariants are about exact bytes; so is this line.
*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
